# interface Node { kind: number; pos: number }  interface Negate extends Node { kind: 1; operand: Node }  interface Num extends Node { kind: 2; value: number }  interface Not extends Node { kind: 3; operand: Node }
# interface NotOrOther extends Node { kind: 3 | 4 }  function NodeObject(this: Node, kind: number, pos: number) { this.kind = kind; this.pos = pos }  (node as Negate).operand  (node as NotOrOther).pos
# Negate and Not have the same names, so the front end sends one layout for both, L2, which every position of either names. Its `kind` only bounds what the two write: 1 to 3.
# What each of them declares comes below it, L4 and L5: types that nothing allocates and no position names. They say which names an object with each literal has.
# A layout is known by the set of its names, so an object made with 1 and one made with 3 have the same layout, L7. Num, whose literal lies between them, has another, L8.
# Each integer that L2 admits has a layout, so no object with the layout of NodeObject itself is one of L2, though the assertion brings it there. L8 has no `operand`, which L2 requires.
# An object made with 4 has the layout of NodeObject, which therefore stays in NotOrOther, beside L7 for 3. Neither tag of NotOrOther is that of L8
